On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:18:27 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> If nothing uses this, we can probably just delete it entirely right?

Yes. v1 had already split the removal in two. Patch 9 dropped only the
Emacs package's symlink to the script, and patch 13 deleted
libexec/mail/maildir-deliver.sh itself. That split made patch 9 look
like it kept the script around, so the v2 cover letter says it
plainly.

In "[PATCH v2 00/16] Move beastie and lab-claude onto lab-mail" [1],
this patch is now "[PATCH v2 11/16] emacs: Stop installing
maildir-deliver.sh" [2]. It removes the link and the package's install
line. "[PATCH v2 15/16] libexec/mail: Remove delivery into the jj
maildir" [3] deletes the script and mail-send.sh. The deletion comes
after the switch patches on purpose. Until patches 9-10 land, Emacs
and lab-process-mail still post through the script, so deleting it
earlier would break posting in the middle of the series.

A few references survive the series, and none of them run on the
switched setup:

- the freebsd-port skill on b/dev still posts with the script. That
  is a separate series, and it needs the git send-email form once
  both land;
- src/claude-skills-old, which is retired;
- a mention in zib/llm-interaction-patterns/research.org, which is
  prose.

